==English==

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|sales|-woman}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|saleswomen}}

# A [[woman]] whose [[occupation]] it is to [[sell]] things.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1911|title=Harper's Magazine
|passage=The task was a novel and not unwholly congenial one, but he finally got the attention of a '''saleswoman''' and made his wishes known.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Paco Underhill|title=Call of the Mall: The Geography of Shopping by the Author of Why We Buy|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=ukQ6QaFjTbQC&pg=PA55&dq=felliniesque&ei=PCW-R8eUJ5HCzAS_k9zeCQ&sig=Bw2ufLPDh8U2mcr8XlKMVthquRc
|passage=We stop walking a second and look around at the spectacle before us. There's something Felliniesque about a department store cosmetics section. You stand here on a Saturday morning, dressed in the standard mall-casual suburban wardrobe, gazing at a chamber glittering with chandeliers, populated by '''saleswomen''' wearing makeup and hair dramatic enough for opening night at La Scala.}}

